title: Normal Pubertal Development
---

# Normal Pubertal Development

- **Physiology of pubertal onset** (from an endocrine perspective) - central process with <u>activation of GnRH pulse generator</u>:
  - **Biochemical basis of pubertal onset** - <u>Kisspeptin</u> from hypothalamus binds to GPR54 (a GPCR on hypothalamic neurons) to secret GnRH
  - **Effects of episodic secretion of hypothalamic GnRH:**
    - <u>Cyclic stimulation of anterior pituitary gonadotrophs</u> - resulting in pulse secretion of FSH and LH, dependeing on frequency of GnRH pulses
    - <u>FSH and LH stimulate gonads</u> - production of estrogen and testosterone by the ovaries and testes respectively
    - <u>Selective -ve HPG axis by gonadal steroids</u> - estrogen and testosterone selectively inhibits HPG axis at pituitary and hypothalamic levels

    
    ![](~/Documents/notes/img/activation_of_pulsatile_gonadotrophin_generator.png)
  - **Effects of gonadal steroids on pubertal development:**
    - <u>Effects of estrogen and testosterone on growth</u> - direct effect on growth plate and indirect action by stimulation of GH release
    - <u>Effects of estrogen</u> - breast development, enlargement of the uterus, and onset of menses
    - <u>Testosterone</u> - penile and scrotal development, and stimulation of growth of hair in androgen-dependent fashion

    
    ![](~/Documents/notes/img/effects_of_estrogen_and_testosterone_on_pubertal_development.png)
- **Other factors modulating pubertal development:**
  - <u>Genetic factors</u> - various genes are a/w pubertal disorders: 
  ![](~/Documents/notes/img/genetic_disorders_of_pubertal_development.png)
  - <u>Peripheral signs</u>:
    - Temporally distant signals - e.g. intrauterine and postnatal growth
    - Temporally recent signals - e.g. BMI and fat mass, insulin sensitivity, gonadal steroid levels
  - <u>Environmental signals</u> - e.g. light, stress, environmental endocrine disruptors
- **Tanner stage** - mean age of sexual development in boys and girls
- **Normal pubertal development in girls** - first sign of pubertal development is breast development accompanied by growth spurt:
  - <u>Growth</u> - accelerates as breast development starts (peak height velocity attained 6-9 after breast budding \[B2\]), and the average girl will grow 6-7cm after menarche
  - <u>Secondary sexual characteristics</u>:
    - **Breast development** as the first sign of pubertal development in girls (accompanied w/ growth spurt), driven by **estrogen** and may be unilateral for several months
    - **Pubic and axillary hair growth** is under the influence of **adrenal androgens**, but usually correlates well with tanner stage
  - <u>Menstruation</u>:
    - Menarche occurs 2-2.5y after breast budding
    - The first menstrual bleedings are anovulatory
    - Time from menarche to regular cycling is about 3y
- **Normal pubertal development in boys:**
  - <u>Growth</u> - peak growth occurs during mid-puberty at mean 13.5 years, corresponding to <u>testicular volume of 10-12 ml</u>
  - <u>Secondary sexual characteristics</u>:
    - **Testicular enlargement** - 1st sign of puberty is increase in testicular volume to 4ml, likely due to enlargement of Sertoli cells, with progressive enlargement of testes during puberty (assessed by Prader orchidometer)
    - **Spermatogenesis** - histological from 11-15y, with sperm found in EMU and ejaculation at around 13y, while adult morphology, motility, and concentration of sperm are found until bone age advances to 17y
    - **Breast development** - physiological breast development early in puberty but typically regress after 2y (this is due to aromatisation of testosterone into estrogen before testosterone reaches concentration that can oppose estrogen)
